-- Q365: Quality Management [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Governance and leadership of the firm","Engagement performance and documentation","Acceptance and continuance of client relationships","Monitoring and remediation of deficiencies"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under SQMS No. 1.30, the acceptance and continuance component requires the firm to evaluate management integrity, the firm''s competence and resources, and ethical compliance before accepting new clients or retaining existing ones. This component directly addresses the prospective client evaluation process.
Wrong (A): Governance and leadership addresses the firm''s culture and tone at the top, not client acceptance decisions.
Wrong (B): Engagement performance governs how individual engagements are conducted, not whether to accept or retain clients.
Wrong (D): Monitoring and remediation involves inspecting completed engagements and correcting deficiencies, not client evaluation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 365;

-- Q1129: Government Auditing Standards [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["40 hours total over the two-year period","80 hours total, with 24 in government auditing","120 hours total over the two-year period","60 hours total, with 20 in government topics"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GAGAS Chapter 4, auditors must complete 80 hours of CPE every two years, with at least 24 hours directly related to the government environment or government auditing. At least 20 hours must be completed in each year of the period.
Wrong (A): 40 hours is the annual requirement under many state boards, not the GAGAS two-year requirement.
Wrong (C): 120 hours exceeds the GAGAS standard; no such requirement exists.
Wrong (D): 60 hours total is below the GAGAS requirement, and 20 hours in government topics understates the 24-hour minimum.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1129;

-- Q1130: Government Auditing Standards [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A report on the entity''s operating efficiency and effectiveness","A report on internal control and on compliance with laws and regulations","A management letter with recommendations for operational improvements","A report comparing the entity''s performance to industry benchmarks"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GAGAS, a financial audit requires additional reports on internal control over financial reporting (including significant deficiencies and material weaknesses) and on compliance with laws, regulations, contracts, and grant agreements. These go beyond the standard GAAS opinion.
Wrong (A): GAGAS does not require a report on operating efficiency as part of a financial audit.
Wrong (C): A management letter is optional and advisory; it is not a required GAGAS report.
Wrong (D): Performance benchmarking is not a required component of GAGAS financial audit reporting.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1130;

-- Q1131: Government Auditing Standards [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["All federal programs must be tested regardless of their size","Only programs with expenditures exceeding $1,000,000 are tested","Programs are classified by expenditure thresholds and risk assessment","The entity selects which federal programs the auditor will test"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the Uniform Guidance (2 CFR 200.518), programs are classified as Type A or Type B based on expenditure thresholds. The auditor then applies risk assessment to determine which are major programs, targeting at least 40% of total federal expenditures (20% for low-risk auditees).
Wrong (A): Not all programs are tested; the risk-based approach selects major programs for compliance testing.
Wrong (B): The $1,000,000 figure is not the defined threshold; Type A classification uses the larger of $750,000 or a percentage of total awards.
Wrong (D): The auditor, not the entity, determines which programs are major programs.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1131;

-- Q1320: Ethics and Independence [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["When the CPA begins fieldwork at the client''s office","When the CPA signs the initial engagement letter","At the earlier of signing the letter or beginning attest services","When the financial statements under audit are issued"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the AICPA Code ET 1.200.010, the professional engagement period begins when the CPA either signs an initial engagement letter or begins to perform any attest services, whichever is earlier. Independence must be maintained throughout this entire period through the date of the final report.
Wrong (A): The period starts before fieldwork; it begins at engagement acceptance or when attest services begin.
Wrong (B): Signing the letter is one possible trigger, but beginning attest services could be earlier.
Wrong (D): Report issuance marks the end of the independence period, not the beginning.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1320;

-- Q1325: Ethics and Independence [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Obtain pre-approval from the AICPA Ethics Division for any conflict","Apply a standard checklist of prohibited activities and relationships","Identify threats, evaluate significance, and apply appropriate safeguards","Automatically decline any engagement where a threat is identified"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the AICPA Code ET 1.000.010, the conceptual framework requires CPAs to identify threats to independence, evaluate their significance, and apply safeguards to eliminate or reduce threats to an acceptable level. If safeguards are insufficient, the CPA must decline the engagement.
Wrong (A): The framework does not require pre-approval from the AICPA; it relies on professional judgment.
Wrong (B): The conceptual framework is principles-based, not a rigid checklist of prohibited items.
Wrong (D): Not all threats require declining the engagement; many can be mitigated through appropriate safeguards.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1325;

-- Q1330: Ethics and Independence [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Self-review threat from evaluating prior work","Advocacy threat from promoting client positions","Undue influence threat from external pressure","Self-interest threat from fee dependence on client"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the AICPA Code ET 1.210.010, a self-interest threat arises when a firm derives a significant portion of its revenue from a single audit client. The firm may be reluctant to challenge management or risk losing the lucrative relationship, impairing objectivity.
Wrong (A): A self-review threat arises when auditing one''s own prior work, not from fee dependence.
Wrong (B): An advocacy threat arises when promoting or representing a client''s position, not from revenue concentration.
Wrong (C): An undue influence threat arises from external pressures, not from the firm''s own financial interest in a client.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1330;

-- Q1353: Professional Responsibilities [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Advertises CPA services through social media platforms","Offers professional services at fees lower than competitors","Provides a second opinion to a prospective audit client","Retains client records as leverage to collect unpaid fees"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the AICPA Code ET 1.400.200, retaining client records to enforce fee collection is an act discreditable to the profession. CPAs must not withhold records the client needs for legal or regulatory obligations, even when fees remain unpaid.
Wrong (A): Advertising CPA services through social media is permissible and not an act discreditable.
Wrong (B): Offering competitive pricing is a normal business practice and does not violate professional standards.
Wrong (C): Providing a second opinion to a prospective client is permitted under the AICPA Code.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1353;

-- Q1364: Professional Responsibilities [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Disregard the predecessor''s workpapers and begin the audit fresh","Report the predecessor auditor to the state board immediately","Withdraw from the engagement due to the client''s fraud history","Evaluate the evidence and extend procedures to assess current impact"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 510.09, the successor auditor should evaluate evidence from the predecessor''s workpapers revealing significant matters such as fraud. AU-C 240.41 requires assessing whether the fraud has current-period implications and communicating findings with those charged with governance under AU-C 260.
Wrong (A): Ignoring predecessor evidence would violate the auditor''s professional obligations to evaluate all relevant information.
Wrong (B): Reporting to the state board may be premature before the auditor understands the scope and current relevance of the fraud.
Wrong (C): Withdrawal is not automatically required; the auditor must first evaluate whether the fraud affects the current engagement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1364;

-- Q1365: Professional Responsibilities [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Maximize shareholder returns for all of their audit clients","Prioritize client interests over all other professional considerations","Serve the public trust including clients, creditors, and government","Ensure that audit fees remain affordable for all companies"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the AICPA Code ET 0.300.030, the public interest principle recognizes that CPAs have obligations to multiple stakeholders including clients, creditors, employers, investors, the business community, and the government. The public trust is paramount.
Wrong (A): Maximizing shareholder returns serves only one stakeholder group and conflicts with the broader public interest obligation.
Wrong (B): Prioritizing client interests alone conflicts with the CPA''s duty to serve the public interest.
Wrong (D): Fee affordability is not a component of the public interest principle under the AICPA Code.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1365;

-- Q1368: Professional Responsibilities [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Confidentiality of client information","Due care in performing professional services","Acts discreditable to the profession","Scope and nature of services provided"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the AICPA Code ET 1.400.001, a felony conviction involving financial fraud constitutes an act discreditable to the profession. Such conduct undermines public confidence in the accounting profession and may require reporting to the state board.
Wrong (A): Confidentiality addresses protecting client information, not criminal conduct by firm personnel.
Wrong (B): Due care relates to competence and diligence in performing services, not to criminal behavior.
Wrong (D): Scope and nature of services addresses what services a CPA may provide, not the conduct of personnel.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1368;

-- Q1374: Professional Responsibilities [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Accept full responsibility for the specialist''s findings as presented","Require the specialist to carry their own professional liability insurance","Disclaim responsibility for audit portions relying on the specialist","Evaluate the specialist''s qualifications and the appropriateness of results"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 620, when using a specialist''s work, the auditor must evaluate the specialist''s professional qualifications, understand the nature and scope of the work performed, and evaluate whether the findings are appropriate and reasonable in relation to audit objectives.
Wrong (A): Accepting findings without evaluation fails the auditor''s responsibility to assess the specialist''s work.
Wrong (B): Requiring liability insurance is not a professional standard requirement for using a specialist.
Wrong (C): The auditor does not disclaim responsibility; the specialist''s work is integrated into the overall audit evidence.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1374;

-- Q1375: Professional Responsibilities [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Confidentiality of client information","Public interest obligations to stakeholders","Acts discreditable to the profession","Competence and due care in practice"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the AICPA Code ET 0.300.060, CPAs must maintain competence in areas where they practice, including keeping current with industry developments and new standards. Failing to update industry knowledge for three years directly violates the competence component of due care.
Wrong (A): Confidentiality relates to protecting client information, not maintaining professional knowledge.
Wrong (B): Public interest is a broader principle about serving stakeholders, not specifically about industry expertise.
Wrong (C): Acts discreditable involves conduct that brings disrepute to the profession, not a failure to maintain knowledge.$EXPL$
